==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|dedly}}, {{m|enm|dedlych}}, {{m|enm|dedlich}}, from {{inh|en|ang|dēadlīċ|pos=adjective}}. Cognate with {{cog|nl|dodelijk}}, {{cog|de|tödlich}}. {{surf|+suf|en|dead|ly|id2=adjectival}}.

The adverb is from {{inh|en|enm|dedliche}}, from {{inh|en|ang|dēadlīċe|pos=adv}}, from the adjective.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdɛd.li/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-deadly.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛdli|s=2}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er|more}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete|rare}} Subject to [[death]]; [[mortal]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1541|author=Rychard [W]hitforde|title=[D]yuers holy inſtrucyons and teachynges very neceſſarye for the helth of mannes ſoule{{...}}|publisher=[W]yllyam Myddylton|location=London|url=https://quod.lib.umich.edu/e/eebo2/A15118.0001.001|page=36|passage={{...}}he ſuffred hym ſelfe to be made mortall and '''dedly''', that innocent & gyltles in hym ſelfe: he myght be ſlayne & deye for the gylty man.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1545|title=[T]woo fruitfull and godly praiers|publisher=Rycharde Lante and Rycharde Bankes|location=London|url=https://quod.lib.umich.edu/e/eebo2/A15118.0001.001|page=36|passage=❧ That when the iournay / of this '''dedly''' life / My ſely ghoſte / hath finiſhed and thence{{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1845|author=Phillip James Bailey|title=Festus: A Poem|publisher=Benjamin B. Mussey|location=Boston|url=https://archive.org/details/festusapoem07bailgoog|page=270|passage=And next we find / Ourselves in Heaven. Even man's '''deadly''' life / Can be there, by God's leave.}}
# Causing death; [[lethal]].
#* {{RQ:Jefferies Amateur Poacher|chapter=1|passage=But then I had the [massive] flintlock by me for protection. ¶{{...}}The linen-press and a chest on the top of it formed, however, a very good gun-carriage; and, thus mounted, aim could be taken out of the window{{nb...}}, and a 'bead' could be drawn upon Molly, the dairymaid, kissing the fogger behind the hedge, little dreaming that the '''deadly''' tube was levelled at them.}}
#* {{RQ:Orwell Nineteen Eighty-Four|chapter=9|page=177|passage={{...}} others search for new and '''deadlier''' gases, or for soluble poisons capable of being produced in such quantities as to destroy the vegetation of whole continents {{...}}}}
# Aiming or willing to destroy; implacable; desperately hostile.
#: {{ux|en|'''deadly''' enemies}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Twelfth Night|III|iv|269|1|{{...}}diſmount thy tucke, be yare in thy preparation, for thy aſſaylant is quick, skilfull and '''deadly'''.}}
# Very [[accurate]] (of aiming with a bow, firearm, etc.).
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1858|work=The Living Age |title=Woolwich Arsenal|author=|volume=57|page=201|passage=Its '''deadly''' aim at vast distances, which condition of the mechanical power brought has made it the dread of the sepoys, who term it "the gun that kills without making any sound," contrasts strangely with the performances of Brown Bess of old, which at any range beyond a hundred yards was so uncertain in its aim that it has been calculated that the soldier shot away the weight in lead of every man that he hit.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1859| title=Iowa Instructor - Volume 1 |author=| page=110| passage=For him the gibbet shall be built; For him the stake prepared: Him shall the scorn and wrath of men Pursue with '''deadly''' aim ; And malice, envy, spite and lies, Shall desecrate his name.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1869| title=The Autumn Wreath: A Selection of Original Poetry |author=George Swann | page=92| passage=Slaves have been freed, religious tests revoked, Bread tax abolished, and free trade secured, Reform twice carried, franchise much enlarged, With '''deadly''' aim to crush foul bribery, And promise given of yet better things.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1888| title=Annual Session of the Baptist Congress for the Discussion of Current Questions|author=| page=188| passage=Possibly some have thought that we were going to make war on some favorite doctrine or political dogma; that we had set brethren to whetting their sword, and drawing their bow for '''deadly''' aim at precious truth; as the wicked Haman had secured a decree that all the Jews should be killed.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006| title=The Sword of Anton |author=Gene Del Vecchio| page=51| passage=The Elf turned and with '''deadly''' accuracy shot an arrow where his nose pointed him. The shaft sliced through the air, pierced the tall grass, and struck the Dwarf's shoes one hundred yards out . . . but the Dwarf was not in them!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2015| title=Her Unexpected Hero |author=Melody Anne | page=288| passage=Was that really her mom grabbing a large Nerf gun from some sort of side holster and aiming it straight at Camden's head? Yes, it was. And the woman was blessed with '''deadly''' accuracy.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2015| title=The Seven Deadly Sins Today|author=Henry Fairlie | page=| passage=There is a wealth of common sense and humanity in that, and perhaps the most unexpected element in the idea of the Seven Deadly Sins is that, although it points with '''deadly''' accuracy to our capacity for evil, it also leaves us with a vivid and strong sense of what it means to be human.}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} Very [[boring]].
#* {{RQ:Chambers Younger Set|VI|passage=“I don't mean all of your friends—only a small proportion—which, however, connects your circle with that '''deadly''', idle, brainless bunch—the insolent chatterers at the opera, the gorged dowagers, the worn-out, passionless men, the enervated matrons of the summer capital,{{nb...}}!”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|author=Oliver Sacks|title=Uncle Tungsten: Memories of a Chemical Boyhood|passage=Now, at school, I was forced to sit in classes, to take notes and exams, to use textbooks that were flat, impersonal, '''deadly'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Gay Lumsden; Donald Lumsden; Carolyn Wiethoff|title=Communicating in Groups and Teams: Sharing Leadership|page=324|passage=Students, of course, know the difference between a '''deadly''' lecture and a stimulating one. An excellent lecturer who maintains a high level of interaction with the audience stimulates thinking and learning.}}
# {{lb|en|informal|Australian Aboriginal|Ireland|Newfoundland}} [[excellent|Excellent]], [[awesome]], [[cool]].

====Usage notes====
* The sense "excellent, awesome, cool" is associated especially with Canada, but is also used in Ireland, and in Australia, where it is especially used by or in connection with Indigenous Australians. It is thought that the term first arrived in Canada via Irish migrants to Newfoundland.

